 17592186045417] [17592186084030 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084055 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084055 #edb/ref 1061 15322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084055 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "69d8dfa2-ca90-55fc-a1e3-baa65975b4db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084055 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084436 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we are given the logarithm $\\log (100).$ If no base is specified, then by default the base is equal to $10.$\n\nIn our example, we have\n\n$$\n\\log(100)=\\log_{10}(100)=\\log_{10}(10^2) = 2.\n$$\n\nIn general, the logarithm with base $10$ is called the **common logarithm**, and is defined as follows:\n\n$a = 10^x$ is equivalent to $\\log{a} = x$\n\nEverything we did with logarithms, we can do with the common logarithm. For example, we can\n\n- convert between exponential and logarithm form,\n\n- evaluate common logarithm expressions, and\n\n- calculate specific values of $\\log a$ using a calculator.\n\nFinally, note that most calculators have a button for the common logarithm. This is usually denoted $\\log_{10}$ or simply $\\log.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084436 #edb/ref 1065 2869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084436 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "624f3e19-2500-53b2-ad06-eeb824d256a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084436 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233673 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186520383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233673 #edb/ref 1069 "q-24882" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233673 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233673 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the value of $\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{2})+\\operatorname{log}⁡(10t)$ at $t=10.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233673 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dbc275e8-af27-549a-94fa-e61e97b3469b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233673 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we substitute $t=10$ into the function. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{2})+\\operatorname{log}⁡(10t) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{2})+\\operatorname{log}⁡(10\\cdot 10) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{2})+\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=2.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{2})+\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{2})=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234203 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186520367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234203 #edb/ref 1069 "q-66543" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234203 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234203 #edb/ref 1071 "${10}^{-2}=\\frac{1}{100}$ can be expressed as" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234203 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2f9714aa-ab20-5f59-96d2-702c693f996b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234203 #edb/ref 1074 "The common logarithm of $a$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a={10}^{x}$ is equivalent to $\\operatorname{log}⁡a=x$\n\nIf we compare ${10}^{-2}=\\frac{1}{100}$ with ${10}^{x}=a,$ we get that $a=\\frac{1}{100}$ and $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation can be expressed as $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{100})=-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520002 #edb/ref 1080 "0.308" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520002 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520002 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "06947b56-6c63-577a-9939-3751fd944d34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520004 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{1}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520004 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520004 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "08c2ab34-4138-5855-8a4e-101598dd9193" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520006 #edb/ref 1080 "-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520006 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520006 #edb/ref 